proposal.text:
{
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "During initial pickup, with target_visits still zero, the policy placed the hover too far from the instructed cube in several scenes; in another scene it commanded a grasp descent roughly 3 cm beyond the cube despite a clear close wrist view. The stored target and button centers were unrelated to these pickup errors. These are waypoint SUFFICIENCY failures in applying the geometric localization rule, not missing historical information. Strengthen the existing cube-localization rule with an explicit calculation: for a selected top-face pixel p=[u,v,1], world-to-camera extrinsics [R|t], and intrinsics K, compute the world camera center C=-R^T t and world ray d=R^T K^-1 p. Intersect the ray with the cube top plane h approximately 0.04 m using X=C+((h-C_z)/d_z)d. Use X_x,X_y for both hover and grasp descent, with their respective commanded TCP heights 0.15 m and 0.02 m. These coordinates are already absolute world coordinates: do not add the current TCP position, apply the camera translation again, swap axes, or substitute the commanded TCP height for h. For the current wrist image use its current reported extrinsics, not the fixed front-camera extrinsics. Identify the top square face separately from darker side faces; where practical, project its four corners onto the top plane and average the resulting world corners to obtain its center. At initial hover use the view with the clearest top-face geometry, checking the other calibrated view for consistency; at descent use the close current wrist view. Derive cube-edge yaw from the projected world corners, choosing an equivalent square-edge alignment nearest the current downward hand orientation. This makes the existing projection and orientation instructions operational; retain the remaining pickup, visit, release, and button rules without adding pickup coordinates or phase fields to memory.",
  "refused": "The failed pickup answers supplied no diagnosis or request for additional memory. Their target_visits values and stored target/button centers agreed with the annotations within the specified tolerances, so no memory filling or comparison changes are warranted. Retain right_target_center despite its infrequent reads: it was consulted when selecting the return to the robot-right target after a left-target arrival. Its purpose is to preserve the fixed target center when current geometry is obscured or cannot be localized reliably; sparse use at precisely those return decisions is not grounds for deletion. It cannot be folded into button_center or target_visits because those fields hold different facts. Keep its existing initialization from the first clearly localizable calibrated marking and subsequent unchanged retention."
}

