      "modify_from": "When target_visits is zero and the instructed cube is not held, use the center of its top square face for both the initial hover and grasp descent, excluding visible side faces from the center estimate. Intersect that pixel's calibrated camera ray with the plane approximately z=0.04 m to obtain world x,y. Approach at that x,y and TCP z=0.15 m with the gripper open, roll approximately pi, pitch zero, and yaw aligned to the top-face edges transformed into world coordinates. Do not infer yaw from a nominal 45-degree rotation or an uncorrected image-edge angle. If already above the cube at hover height, recompute the top-face center from the current calibrated wrist image and command the resulting world x,y at TCP z=0.02 m and close. Neither the hover height nor the grasp TCP height is the projection plane. Do not copy the reported hover TCP x,y merely because it is approximately aligned; correct ordinary hover tracking error in the descent.",
      "modify_to": "When target_visits is zero and the instructed cube is not held, localize its top square face for both the initial hover and grasp descent, excluding visible side faces. For a selected top-face pixel p=[u,v,1], use that image's intrinsics K and world-to-camera extrinsics [R|t] to compute the world camera center C=-R^T t and world ray d=R^T K^-1 p. Intersect with the cube top plane h approximately 0.04 m: X=C+((h-C_z)/d_z)d. Where the four top-face corners can be identified, project each corner this way and average the resulting world points to obtain the face center; otherwise project the identified top-face center. The resulting x,y are absolute world coordinates: do not add the current TCP position, apply camera translation again, or swap axes. Use the current reported wrist-camera extrinsics for the current wrist image, not the fixed front-camera extrinsics. For the initial hover, use the calibrated view with the clearest top-face geometry and check the other view for consistency. Approach at the resulting world x,y and TCP z=0.15 m with the gripper open, roll approximately pi and pitch zero. Derive yaw from top-face edges projected into world coordinates, choosing an equivalent square-edge alignment nearest the current downward hand orientation; do not use a nominal 45-degree rotation or an uncorrected image-edge angle. If already above the cube at hover height, recompute the face center from the close current calibrated wrist image and command the resulting world x,y at TCP z=0.02 m and close. Neither commanded TCP height replaces h in the projection calculation. Do not copy the reported hover TCP x,y merely because it is approximately aligned; correct ordinary hover tracking error in the descent."
    }
  ],
  "refused": "No memory changes or episode-specific pickup coordinates are committed: the reported failures concern current-view geometric calculation, not missing history. Retain both stored centers and all visit-count rules. Infrequent reads do not overturn the earlier demonstrated need for retained locations during occlusion. No earlier edit is undone; the pickup clarification makes the existing top-face projection rule explicit, while target, release, and button rules remain unchanged."
}
